.TH NC 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
nc \- send and receive over TCP or UDP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nc
.RB [ \-u ]
.RB [ \-b ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR port ]
.I address port
.br
.B nc
.RB [ \-u ]
.B \-l
.RB [ \-p ]
.I port
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nc
(netcat) sends what it reads from standard input over the network,
through the computer's network cards, and writes what comes back to
standard output.
.I address
is a network card's address (its id, as
.B lsdev \-i
shows it, with or without its
.B id:
prefix).
.PP
Over TCP (the default) it connects to
.I port
at
.IR address ,
or with
.B \-l
listens on
.I port
and takes one connection. Then it sends what it reads and writes what
comes, until the other side ends; at the end of its input it says it's done
sending, so the other side reads its end.
.PP
With
.BR \-u ,
UDP: each read (a line, from a terminal) is a datagram to
.I port
at
.IR address ,
or
.B broadcast
for every card the computer's cards reach (a wired card's cables, a WLAN
card's range). What comes back to its port is written out. It ends when
standard input ends. With
.BR \-l ,
it writes each datagram that comes, and sends what it reads to whoever
sent the last one, until it's stopped (^C).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-u
UDP, not TCP.
.TP
.B \-l
Listens on
.IR port .
.TP
.BI \-p " port"
The port it sends from (or listens on, with
.BR \-l ).
Without it, a free one is given (32768 to 60999).
.TP
.B \-b
Taken, and needed for nothing: mcos sends to
.B broadcast
without SO_BROADCAST set.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when it's done; 1 for a bad option or port, or when it can't connect or
send (no network card: "Network is unreachable"; no card took it: "No route
to host"; nothing listening: "Connection refused").
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
nc \-l 7 > /tmp/heard
nc 1b9c0e\-6f0c\-4e0f\-9a43\-7d2f3c1a5e88 7 < notes
nc \-ul 7 > /tmp/heard
echo hello | nc \-u 1b9c0e6a\-6f0c\-4e0f\-9a43\-7d2f3c1a5e88 7
echo all | nc \-ub broadcast 7
.fi
.SH NOTES
mcos has UDP and TCP but no IP: an address is a network card's, and the
network is what the cards reach, with nothing routed between networks (a
wired card and a WLAN card are two networks). A card never drops a frame:
the other card takes it, or the sender hears why not at once ("No buffer
space available": it's busy, its queue full or no program taking data there;
"No route to host": no card there in reach), and nothing tries again but
the software. So a datagram that's sent reached the other card; it's lost
there only when no program has its port, or its socket's queue is full, as
UDP's are. TCP loses nothing: it tries again what wasn't taken, or wasn't
acknowledged. A datagram or a
connection to the computer's own card, or a broadcast, reaches its own
programs too.
.PP
GNU and OpenBSD netcat over UDP stay after standard input ends, until
.B \-q
or
.B \-w
says; this one ends at once, as with
.BR "\-q 0" .
Over TCP it says it's done sending at the end of its input, as OpenBSD's
.B \-N
does.
.PP
A network card can turn its computer on while it's off (Wake\-on\-LAN):
a frame for it holding its wake message, anywhere in the data, presses the
power button. A program sets the message (an empty one: none) with the
card's own method, an ioctl on its /dev file:
.PP
.nf
local sys = require("sys")
sys.ioctl(sys.open("/dev/network0"), "setWake", "letmein")
.fi
.PP
Then, from another computer:
.B echo letmein | nc \-ub broadcast 9
(port 9, discard, is the usual one; any will do). Interfaces are named as
Linux's:
.B eth0
for the first wired card,
.B wlan0
for the first WLAN card.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lsdev (1)
